course: dictionary
lesson: 3419
title: Strong words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words carry a lot of weight, and the sentence around them shows how heavy they are. «Expulsion, being sent away from a school for good, is very rare and serious.» The clues are sent away, for good, rare and serious. Together they tell you that expulsion is a big and final thing.
explain: Expulsion means making someone leave a school, a club or a country for ever, as a punishment. It can also mean forcing something out, such as air from the lungs. To choose the right meaning, swap it into the sentence. «The expulsion of air» is about pushing air out, not about punishment.
explain: Both meanings share one idea: something is forced out. Ask yourself what is being pushed out, and where it is going. If it is a person leaving a place as a punishment, use the school meaning. If it is air leaving a body, use the science meaning.
fact: Expulsion means making someone leave for ever, as a punishment, as in «expulsion from school».
fact: Expulsion also means forcing something out, as in «the expulsion of air» from the lungs.
fact: Expulsion is the most serious punishment in school rules: being sent away for good.
fact: «Face expulsion» means be at risk of being sent away for good.
exercise: «Expulsion, being sent away from a school for good, is very rare and serious.» What does expulsion mean here? | Being moved to a new classroom | Being given a day off to play | *Being sent away from a school for good | Being praised in assembly
exercise: In science, the expulsion of air means the air is what? | *Pushed out of the lungs | Stored in a balloon for later | Heated until it turns to water | Filling the room with sound
exercise: Which phrase means being sent away from school for good? | the expulsion of air | the school bell | *expulsion from school | a school trip
test: «The expulsion of air from the lungs happens every time you breathe out.» What does expulsion mean in this sentence? | Being sent away for good | *Forcing something out | A punishment in school | Taking a deep breath in
test: The club rules say a member can face expulsion. What does face expulsion mean? | Be told to tidy up the room | Be given a prize for good behaviour | *Risk being made to leave the club for good | Be asked to join another club
exam: Which meaning fits best in «Expulsion from the country was a harsh punishment in history»? | Welcoming visitors at the border | *Making someone leave a country for ever as a punishment | Forcing air out of the lungs | Building a new road
